+Revision IDs are generated on the tablet so that a worker can make several edits in a row while offline: each later edit names the earlier, not-yet-submitted revision as its base. Such a chain must be submitted in order.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+The store authenticates the worker, then validates field types and that the plot ID resolves in the site catalog (active or archived). It answers with one of:
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+| Outcome | When | Effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| Accepted, visit `settled` | The base is the visit's current revision, or the visit is new | The revision is stored and becomes the current revision |
+| Accepted, visit `contested` | The base is any other known revision of this visit, or the visit already exists and no base is named | The revision is stored as a competing revision; the response lists all competing revision IDs |
+| Rejected: invalid | A field or plot reference fails validation | Nothing is stored; the response names the failing fields |
+| Rejected: unknown base | The base revision ID is not a revision of this visit | Nothing is stored |
+| Rejected: key conflict | The revision ID was already accepted with a different payload | Nothing is stored; the original revision is unchanged |
+
+A retry with the same revision ID and identical payload returns the original acceptance result, with the visit's state as it is at the time of the retry.
+
+A worker who edits their own competing revision supersedes only that revision; the visit stays `contested`. An edit based on a revision that was superseded while the tablet was offline — including one settled by a coordinator in the meantime — is accepted as a competing revision and makes the visit `contested` again. A rejection is always explicit, and the tablet still holds the draft.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval for a visit returns its state and:
+
+- when `settled`, the current revision in full, with its revision ID, author, and receipt time;
+- when `contested`, no current revision, and each competing revision in full with its revision ID, base revision ID, author, and receipt time.
+
+History retrieval returns every revision of the visit, including superseded ones, each with its base revision ID, author, receipt time, and what superseded it (a later revision or a resolution), together with every resolution recorded for the visit. This is the record from which any exported value can be traced back to the submissions behind it.
+
+The store also lists the visits that are currently `contested`.
+
+## Resolution
+
+Only a coordinator can resolve a contested visit. A resolution names every competing revision ID it settles and gives one of two outcomes:
+
+- **Keep one**: one of the named competing revisions becomes the current revision.
+- **Replace**: the coordinator submits a new complete revision, with its own client-generated revision ID, which becomes the current revision. It is stored as a revision authored by the coordinator.
+
+In both cases the other named revisions are superseded by the resolution and remain in history. The store records the coordinator's identity, the time, the revision IDs settled, and the resulting current revision.
+
+A resolution must name exactly the visit's competing revisions at the time it is applied. If another competing revision arrived after the coordinator loaded the visit, the resolution is rejected and the response lists the present competing revisions, so that no submitted revision is settled without having been looked at. A resolution of a visit that is already `settled` is rejected the same way.
+
+## Contract for Dependent Nodes
+
+Tablet-sync and review-export take their concurrency semantics from this node and link to it rather than restating them.
+
+- **Tablet-sync** generates visit and revision IDs, keeps the base revision ID for each local draft, submits chained offline edits in order, and presents the two accepted outcomes and three rejections above to field workers. It sizes local storage around complete visit payloads plus those identifiers; it keeps no base snapshot of field values.
+- **Review-export** builds its review queue from the list of contested visits, compares competing revisions as whole visits using current-state and history retrieval, and settles them through the resolution operation. Only `settled` visits have a current revision, so only they can supply values to the partner CSV.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| How concurrent edits to one visit become a current record | Whole-visit revisions: concurrent edits become competing complete revisions that a coordinator resolves | Field-aware three-way merge: combine edits to different fields automatically and review only overlapping ones | A merge rule can combine scientifically related edits into a visit nobody observed, without anyone noticing; the larger review queue is accepted in exchange for having no merge rules to maintain. Full criteria, both options, and when to reopen: [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Who generates revision IDs | The tablet | Server-assigned revision numbers | A tablet offline for 48 hours must be able to base a second edit on a first that the server has not yet seen. |
+| Idempotency key | The revision ID | A separate key alongside the revision ID | A retry is a resubmission of the same revision; one identifier removes the case where the two disagree. |
+| State of a contested visit | No current revision until resolved | Keep serving the last revision that was current; serve the earliest- or latest-received competing revision | Any stand-in value would either hide a submitted edit or let arrival order pick a winner. |
+| Edit based on a superseded revision | Accepted as a competing revision | Reject and ask the worker to redo the edit on the current revision | Rejecting would discard a submitted edit made in good faith offline. |
+| Resolution scope | Must name every competing revision present when applied | Resolve pairs; apply to whatever was competing when the coordinator loaded the visit | Guarantees each competing revision was in front of the coordinator when it was settled. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
